\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Conditional propagators and induced vertices}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For an \vocabk{interacting selected background}{fdip-005Y}, put
#{r=\operatorname{rank}P}. Write #{\iota_X:X\to H} and
#{\iota_Y:Y\to H} for the inclusions of the image and kernel of #{P}.
Define
##{\begin{aligned}
A&=\iota_X^{\dagger}\,Q\,\iota_X,&K&=\iota_X^{\dagger}\,Q\,\iota_Y,\\
D&=\iota_Y^{\dagger}\,Q\,\iota_Y>0,&S&=A-KD^{-1}K^{\dagger}.
\end{aligned}}
The eliminated component, conditionally on the retained vector
#{x\in X}, has law
##{y=\mu(x)+\eta,\qquad
   \mu(x)=-D^{-1}K^{\dagger}x,\qquad \eta\sim N(0,D^{-1}).}
The full conditional mean and noise covariance are
##{h(x)=\iota_Xx-\iota_YD^{-1}K^{\dagger}x,\qquad
   C=\iota_YD^{-1}\iota_Y^{\dagger}.}
The map #{h} is the harmonic extension: #{\iota_X^{\dagger}h(x)=x} and
#{\iota_Y^{\dagger}Qh(x)=0}. It is the unique minimizer of the quadratic
energy among fields whose retained component is #{x}. Orthonormal
coordinates, the coordinate-selector formulas and the quartic
components are given in \ref{fdip-00LS}.}

\p{Write #{F_\lambda(x)=F_{b,\mathcal V}(\lambda,x)}, with
#{x\in X}. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
a(x)&=\mathbb E\mathcal V(h(x)+\iota_Y\eta),\\
b(x)&=\operatorname{Var}\bigl(\mathcal V(h(x)+\iota_Y\eta)\bigr),\\
F_\lambda(x)&=\lambda a(x)-\frac{\lambda^2}{2}b(x)
                   +O(\lambda^3).
\end{aligned}}
Thus external mean factors in the conditional moment expansion use
#{h(x)}, and each internal pairing of eliminated vectors uses the
covariance #{D^{-1}} on #{Y}. On the full field that pairing is the
operator #{C}. The eliminated mean #{\mu} alone omits the retained
part of #{h}. These are the background-dependent Feynman rules for
this fiber integral.}

\proof{
\p{Choose orthonormal basis maps #{B:\mathbb R^r\to X} and
#{B_\perp:\mathbb R^{(\dim H)-r}\to Y}, and use them as the inclusions
#{\iota_X} and #{\iota_Y} for this calculation. A full field is
#{\phi=Bx+B_\perp y}. Orthogonality preserves the volume and gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\langle\phi,Q\phi\rangle
 &=x^TAx+2x^TKy+y^TDy\\
 &=x^TSx+(y+D^{-1}K^Tx)^TD(y+D^{-1}K^Tx).
\end{aligned}}
The second term is minimized uniquely at #{y=\mu(x)}. Normalizing
its density proves the conditional law at every #{x}. Writing
#{v_x(y)=Bx+B_\perp y}, it also gives
##{\begin{aligned}
c_D&=\frac{(2\pi)^{((\dim H)-r)/2}}{\sqrt{\det D}},\\
\int_{\mathbb R^{(\dim H)-r}}&\ue^{-\frac12\langle v_x(y),Qv_x(y)\rangle}
        \ue^{-\lambda\mathcal V(v_x(y))}\,dy\\
 &=c_D\ue^{-\frac12x^TSx}\ue^{-F_\lambda(x)}.
\end{aligned}}
The last equality means equality after integrating each polynomial
coefficient. Hence it retains the scalar and degree-zero terms as
well as the induced polynomial vertices.}

\p{The moment series starts with
#{1-\lambda a+\lambda^2\mathbb E[\mathcal V^2\mid x]/2+O(\lambda^3)}.
Applying #{-\log(1+z)=-z+z^2/2+O(z^3)} gives the displayed
variance coefficient. In each moment, expand around #{h(x)}.
Odd centered Gaussian moments vanish; every even moment is a sum
over pairings, with one #{D^{-1}} entry per pair in eliminated
coordinates. This is
\citet{v2, Theorem 3.1 and Example 3.2, p. 29}{etingof2024mathematical}
applied to the positive form #{D}. For a quartic
#{\mathcal V(\phi)=T(\phi,\phi,\phi,\phi)/24}, the first coefficient
has degrees #{4,2,0}. In the variance, pairings with no contraction
between the two copies cancel, leaving degrees among #{6,4,2,0},
as shown in \ref{fdip-0046}. The coefficients #{6} and #{3} are
the components in \ref{fdip-00LS}. All these claims concern finite
polynomial coefficients.}
}

\p{The massive lattice analogue is
\citet{Theorem 8.26, p. 399}{friedli2017statistical}: on a finite
domain of #{\mathbb Z^n}, the conditional field has the massive
harmonic mean determined by its boundary values and a killed-walk
Green covariance. That theorem uses its normalized lattice Laplacian;
it does not supply the arbitrary-projector argument above. The
massless finite-domain analogue is Theorem 8.17, p. 391 of the same
source.}

\p{The conditional density in the proof is the explicit Gaussian
kernel at every retained value #{x}. A coordinate declaration of
the regular conditional law identifies that law only almost
everywhere in the observed marginal. The two scopes, the quartic
components and the singular full covariance are compared in
\ref{fdip-00LS}.}
